%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Programming in Assembler}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\nsl{The method of programming a microcontroller is little bit different from that used for programming of microprocessors. In the case of microcontrollers, the programming is usually done using the ``cross compilation'' technique. In this method, all the necessary tools for compiling are installed on a PC (i.e. editor, compiler, linker etc.) and the executable file generated is then downloaded to the target system. Here, the target system (e.g. ARM-Cortex-A8) has a completely different processing core than the host PC (which employs e.g. an Intel Processor).
\newline
Another method involves development on the target directly and doesn't have a download mechanism. The target processor and the processor for the development system is the same and a loader of an available operating system loads the executable file and starts the program.
\newline
A next important difference between the development of programs for microcontroller and microprocessor is the opportunity to use an operating system or not. In nearly every application which uses a microprocessor there is support from an operating system (OS). Simple input/output-operations, memory allocation and mathematical libraries would be offered through the operating system. In the case of a microcontroller you have to program all these functions. 
\newline
The microcontroller ARM-Cortex-A8 is a processor just on the border between microprocessors and microcontrollers. From the point of a programmer it is a typical microprocessor, from the point of a hardware designer it is a highly sophisticated microcontroller. So in this chapter we don't find the specific restrictions which are typical for other microcontrollers. Starting with an overview about all the registers (register model) from the different parts of this controller we will progress to assembler programming aspects. The focus is to understand the specific aspects of this controller and not to write big assembler programs. These could be better done through C-programs.
}

\begin{description}
\item[{\rot\bf Register model}]~\\[-3ex]
\bigskip
\bi
	\ite the register model of all ARM processors first introduce the ALU register set
	\ite the ALU registers are used for data operations and for address calculations
	\ite a few register are only visible in the different processor mode (like user mode, system mode, supervisor mode .. )
	\ite other processor units (like FPU, NEON) use there one register sets 
	\ite register models are important for the development of assembler programs (a C-compiler tries to hide the most aspects of registers).
	\ite registers are part of the core (not mapped in the address space of the memory).
	\bigskip
	\ite register model for integer operations (core register).
	\bi 
		\ite register r0 - r12 used for data- and address-operation
		\ite register r13 \pr stack (address register for the stack)
		\ite register r14 \pr link (address register for the return address of a subroutine)
		\ite register r15 \pr pc (address register for the next instruction)
		\ite the access to a data-register could be done as a Byte (b), Halfword (h) (16-bit) or Word (32-bit) (default).
		\ite the access to an address-register only a Word is possible.
	\ei 
\ei
\os{\newpage}
\begin{center}
\epsfig{file=figures/Fig_4_1_RegisterSet.png,width=0.8\textwidth}\\
Fig 4.1 Integer Registers
\end{center}
\os{\newpage}
\bi 
	\ite CPSR \pr \underline{C}ommon \underline{P}rocessor \underline{S}tatus \underline{R}egister 
	\ite 32 bit register (with 6 different copies in SPSR, depends on the different modes of the processor).

\ei
\begin{center}
\epsfig{file=figures/Fig_4_2_CPSR.png,width=0.8\textwidth}\\
Fig 4.2 Processor Status Word
\end{center}

\bi
	\ite N \pr Negative flag (set if the most significant bit of the result is set; otherwise cleared)
	\ite Z \pr Zero flag (set if the result equals zero; otherwise cleared)..
	\ite C \pr Carry flag (set if a carry out of the most significant bit of the operand occurs for an addition, or if a borrow occurs in a subtraction; otherwise cleared); for unsigned numbers.
	\ite V \pr Overflow flag (set if an arithmetic overflow occurs implying that the result cannot be represented in the operand size; otherwise cleared); for signed numbers.
	\ite Q \pr Sticky Overflow (not changed after every arithmetic operations; explicitly changed with a MSR instruction)

	\os{\bigskip}

	\ite flags could be changed after all instructions (including transfer instructions).
	\ite flags are used in conditional branch instructions (the instruction which affected a flag and the appropriate branch instruction should follow each other).
	\ite flags could also used in conditional transfer instructions (typical feature of RISC-Processors)
\ei


\os{\newpage}
\item[{\rot\bf Addressing modes}]~\\[-3ex]
\os{\bigskip}
\bi 
	\ite addressing modes could be separated in five groups
	\bigskip
	\ite {\rot\bf Register addressing}
	\bi 
		\ite name of the register coded in the instruction
		\\ \begin{tabular}{l l}
		      LDR r0,r1 &;load the contents form r1 to r0 (32 bit, default) \\
		      LDRB r2,r0 &;load the least significant byte from r0 to r2 \\
			& ;(least significant position)\\
		      LDRH r3,r2 &;load the least significant halfword from r2 to r3\\ 
			& ;(same position in r3)\\
			& \\
		      SDR r0,r1 &;store the contents form r0 to r1 (32 bit, default) \\
		      SDRB r2,r0 &;store the least significant byte from r2 to r0\\ 
			& ;(least significant position)\\
		      SDRH r3,r2 &;store the least significant halfword from r3 to r2 (same position in r2)\\
			& \\
		      MOV r0,r1 & ;transfer contents form r0 to r1 (allways 32 bit) \\
		      MOVS r0,r1 & ;transfer contents form r0 to r1 (allways 32 bit)\\ 
			& ;and set the Z- and N-flag 
		     \end{tabular}
		\ite RISC-like register instructions
		\\ \begin{tabular}{l l}
		      LDR r15,r14 &;load PC with the contents of the link register\\
			& ;\pr same as RET-instruction\\
		      MSR r4,CPSR &;move the contents of CPSR to r4 (32 bit)\\
		      MRS CPSR\_f,r4 &;move the flag-field \\
			& ;(5 most significant bits \pr N-, Z-, C-, V-, Q-flag)\\ 
		   \end{tabular}

	\ei
	\os{\newpage}
	\ite {\rot\bf Immediate addressing}
	\bi 
		\ite instruction contains the data
		\ite the immediate value is signed (-4095 to +4096 for LDR-instruction) 
		\ite this type of addressing is useful for source addressing only
		\\ \begin{tabular}{l l}
		      LDR r0,\#0x100 & ;transfer the value 100 (hex)to r0 (32 bit)\\
		      LDR r0,\#0x-100 & ;transfer the value -100 (hex)to r0 (32 bit)\\
			& \\
		      LDR r0,=\#0x2000 & ;transfer the value 2000 (hex)\\ 
			& ;to r0 (pseudo-instruction)\\
			& ;LDR r0,=\#0x2000 \pr LDR r0,[pc,offset\_to\_litpool]\\
			& ;...\\
			& ;litpool DCD 0x2000
		     \end{tabular}

	\ei
	\os{\newpage}
	\ite {\rot\bf Direct addressing}
	\bi 
		\ite instruction contains the address of a data
		\ite not directly implemented, an direct or absolut address could be given with a pseudo-instruction
		\\ \begin{tabular}{l l}
		      LDR r0,=label &;load the register r0 with address of label\\
			& ;LDR r0=label \pr LDR r0,[pc,offset\_to\_litpool]\\
			& ;...\\
			& ;litpool DCD label;\\
		     \end{tabular}

	\ei
	\os{\newpage}
	\ite {\rot\bf Indirect addressing}
	\bi 
		\ite use one of the registers r0 - r12.
		\ite address registers r13 - a15 are special address registers.
		\ite address registers are always 32-bit registers.
		\ite indirect addressing modes operate in different versions.
		\bigskip
		\ite {\rot\bf Indirect addressing standard}
		\\ \begin{tabular}{l l}
		      LDR r1,[r0] & ;load the register r1 with contents\\
			& ;of address where r0 points to\\
		      SDR r2,[r0] & ;store the register r2 to the address\\
			& ;where r0 points to\\
		     \end{tabular}
		\os{\newpage}
		\ite {\rot\bf Indirect addressing with index and offset}
		\ite address calculation with an address register and an additional register and a offset
		\ite the additional register and the offset are signed.
		\\ \begin{tabular}{l l}
		      LDR r0,[r1,r2,0x20] &;load register r0 from the address given by\\ 
			& ;r1 + r2 + 0x20\\
		      SDR r0,[r1,-r2,-0x20] &;store register r0 at the address given by \\
			& ;r1 - r2 - 0x20\\ 
		     \end{tabular}
		\bigskip
		\ite {\rot\bf Indirect addressing with index,offset and update}
		\ite address calculation with an address register and an additional register and a offset
		\ite the additional register and the offset are signed.
		\ite after the transfer the first mentioned address register are updated 
		\\ \begin{tabular}{l l}
		      LDR r0,[r1,r2,0x20]! &;load register r0 from the address given by \\
			& ;r1 + r2 + 0x20, r1 updated\\ 
		      SDR r0,[r1,-r2,-0x20]! &;store register r0 at the address given by \\
			& ;r1 - r2 - 0x20, r1 updated\\
		     \end{tabular}
		\os{\newpage}

		\ite{\rot\bf post increment / pre decrement}
		\ite load and store registers from stack
		\\\begin{tabular}{l l}
		      LDMFA r13!,\{r0 - r12\} & ;load stackframe (r13) to registers r0 - r12 \\
			& ;full asscending stack\\
			& ;increment register r13 and store\\ 
			& ;the last position of the stack in r13 \\
			& ;(pop-operation on Intel processors)\\
			& \\
		     STMFD r13!\{r0 - r12\}, & ;generate a stackframe r0 - r12 \\
			& ;full descending stack\\
			& ;save registers r0 - r12 on the stack\\
			& (push-operation on Intel processors)\\
			& \\
		     STMFD  r13!,\{r4 - r12,LR\} & ;push registers r4 - r12 to the stack\\
			& ;and place the return address (LR)\\
			& ;on the stack\\
		     LDMFA r13!,\{r4 - r12,PC\} & pop registers r 4 - r12 from the stack\\
			& ;and jump back to the calling routine\\
		     \end{tabular}
		\os{\newpage}
		\ite post inrement and predecrement addressing mode work which other register as the stack also
		\\\begin{tabular}{l l}
		      LDMIA / STMIA... & ;increment after transfer\\
		      LDMIB / STMIB... & ;increment before transfer\\
		      LDMDA / STMDA... & ;decrement after transfer\\
		      LDMDB / STMDB... & ;decrement before transfer\\
		 
		 \end{tabular}
		\bigskip
		 
	\ei
	\os{\newpage}
	\ite {\rot\bf Relative addressing}
	\bi 
		\ite the program counter (PC) works as an address register.
		\ite this addressing mode has to be selected for position independent programs.
		\ite only useful with an offset.
		\ite relative addressing modes operate in different versions (index register).
		\bigskip
		\ite {\rot\bf Relative addressing with an offset}
		\ite the offset is signed value.
		\\ \begin{tabular}{l l}
		      LDR r0,[PC,0x20] & ;load the register r0 with contents\\
			& ;of address where (PC + 20) points to\\
		     \end{tabular}
		\bigskip
		\ite easier use of relative the relative addressing mode with the pseudo instruction ADR
		\\\begin{tabular}{l l}
		      ADR r0,label & ;load the register r0 with the \\
			& ;relative address of label\\
		 \end{tabular}
	\ei
\ei
\os{\newpage}
\bi 
	\ite Classification of the instruction set
	\\
	\bi 
		\ite transfer instructions
		\\
		\ite integer arithmetic instructions
		\\
		\ite floating-point arithmetic instructions
		\\
		\ite logical instructions
		\\
		\ite bit manipulation instructions
		\\
		\ite control instructions
		\\
		\ite other
	\ei
\ei
	
\end{description}

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Programming in C}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\begin{description}
\item[{\rot\bf Specification}]~\\[-3ex]
\bi 
	\ite Cross compilation:
	\ite use of a special toolchain to build programs for ARM-Cortex-A8
	\bi 
		\ite compiler arm-none-linux-gnueabi-gcc.
		\ite version for the ``standard''-gcc (generates output for the ARM-Cortex-processor)
		\ite in the case of cross compilation there is mostly support from an operating system. This means it makes no sense to use the MMU features of the processor.
		\ite the output of the compiler depends on the position which has to be specified in the linker.
		\ite if the linker doesn't generate a position depend output a additional tool necessary
		\bi
		   \ite objdump (mostly part of a build package) 
		\ei    
		\ite this output format is indicated by the endings ``.bin''.
		\ite  to download these programs in the target convert the ``.bin''-programs to a ``.srec''-format or a ``.ihex''-format if using RS-232-Interface.
	\ei
	\os{\newpage}
	\ite Development in the target system
	\bi	
		\ite use of the same compiler (arm-none-linux-gnueabi-gcc) as in the case with cross compilation.
		\ite the development has to be done in the target system (longer development cycles).
		\ite the executable file has to be generated position independent(controlled by a special linker command).
		\ite in the case ARM-Cortex-processor the MMU is active.
		\ite the output of the linker is build as .elf-file (elf - \underline{e}xecutable and \underline{l}inkable \underline{f}ormat) and could directly loaded from the operating system. 
	\ei
	\bigskip
	\ite Development with the same operating system in the target and in the cross-system
	\bi 
		\ite use of Linux as operating system on the target.
		\ite implementation of an NFS-server (\underline{n}etwork \underline{f}ile \underline{s}ystem) on the cross-system. 
		\ite mount the ``working directory'' of the cross-system in the target and direct load and execution is possible.
		\ite shorter development cycles as in the cross-compilation.
		\ite uses the advantages of an operating system.
	\ei
\ei
\os{\newpage}
\item[{\rot\bf Special features}]~\\[-3ex]
\bi 
	\ite no restriction in the use of data types.
	\ite the type float and double also supported (the basic arithmetic steps like add, sub, mul, and div directly supported from the FPU, more complex floating point arithmetic expressions like sin(x), cos(x), \begin{math} x^{y} \end{math} supported from a special library).
	\bigskip
	\ite access to registers (and memory) could be done in two different methods:
	\bi 
		\ite cross-compilation without the use on the MMU (independent of the OS, the memory here is managed by the complier).
		\ite set a pointer to the register (type of pointer depends on the register).
		\\ \begin{tabular}{l l}
		  \#define I/O\_Peripherals 0xF2000000 & /* Base address of */ \\
					& /* all I/O Address */ \\
		    & \\
		  (*unsigned int *)(I/O\_Peripherals) = 0x1234; & /* Set Register */ \\
		   \end{tabular}
		\ite program runs in the ``user-mode'' of the processor.
		\bigskip
		\ite development in the target with the use of the MMU.
		\ite program have to be developed in the ``kernel-mode''.
	\ei
	
\ei
\os{\newpage}
\item[{\rot\bf Parameter Passing Conventions}]~\\[-3ex]
\bi 
	\ite C-Programs and Assembler-Programs could be combined without any restrictions.
	\ite this feature is based on a similar concept about passing parameters (C-parameter passing conventions).
	\bi 
		\ite specification of the return value of a function (always passed in registers r0, f0, d0, s0 as follows)
		\\ \begin{tabular}{l l}
		    Byte (char, unsigned char) & \pr r0[7..0] \\
		    Halfword (short, unsigned short) & \pr r0[15..0] \\
		    Word (int, unsigned int, pointers) & \pr r0 \\
		    Float & \pr f0, s0 \\
		    Double & \pr d0 \\	
		   \end{tabular}
\os{\newpage}
		\ite the calling parameter are passed on the registers r0 - r3 and the stack (from left to right)
		\ite  {Register names and there use}\\
\os{\newpage}
		\begin{center}
		  \begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|}
		    \hline
		    Register &  Synonym & Use\\
		    \hline
		    r15 & PC & Program counter\\
		    \hline
		    r14 & LR & Link register \\
		    \hline
		    r13 & SP & Stack pointer \\
		    \hline
		    r12 & IP & Intra-procedure-call\\
		    \hline
		    r11 & v8 & Variable register\\
		    \hline
		    r10 & v7 & Variable register \\
		    \hline
		    r9 & v6 & Variable register\\
		    \hline
		    r8 & v5 & Variable register \\
		    \hline
		    r7 & v4 & Variable register\\
		    \hline
		    r6 & v3 & Variable register \\
		    \hline
		    r5 & v2 & Variable register\\
		    \hline
		    r4 & v1 & Variable Register \\
		    \hline
		    r3 & a4 & Argument register 4\\
		    \hline
		    r2 & a3 & Argument register 3\\
		    \hline
		    r1 & a2 & Argument register 2\\
		    \hline
		    r0 & a1 & Argument register 1\\
		    \hline
		  \end{tabular}
		\end{center}

		\ite example int myfunc(int i, int j, int k, int l, int m ); /* myfunc = i + j + k + l + m */
		\newline
		\begin{center}
		Preparation of the Stack \\
		\begin{tabular}{c}
		   
		 SP \pr \\
		\end{tabular}
		\begin{tabular}{|c|}		  
		  \hline
		  int m \\
		  \hline
		\end{tabular}\\
		Transfer of the parameters with registers\\
		\begin{tabular}{|c|c|}
		  \hline 
		  r3 & int l\\
		  \hline 
		  r2 & int k\\
		  \hline 
		  r1 & int j\\
		  \hline 
		  r0 & int i\\
		  \hline 
		\end{tabular}
		\end{center}
\os{\newpage}
		\ite Program code \\
		\ite ;int myfunc(int i, int j, int k, int l, int m)\\ 
		\begin{tabular}{l l l}
		  EXPORT &myfunc & \\
		  AREA &myfunc, CODE, READONLY &\\
		  myfunc:&ADD r0,r0,r1 &;r0 = r0 + r1\\
		  &ADD r0,r0,r2 &;r0 = r0 + r2\\
		  &ADD r0,r0,r3 &;r0 = r0 + r3\\
		  &LDR r3, [sp] &;load int m from stack\\
		  &ADD r0,r0,r3 &;result in r0 (return value)\\
		  &ADD sp,sp,\#4 &;remove int m from stack\\
		  &LDR pc,lr &;back to the calling function\\
		  END & & \\
		\end{tabular}


		\ite if there are additional local variable necessary, they could placed on the stack.
		\ite before leave every reservation on the stack (parameter and local variable) have to be removed

	\ei 
\ei
\end{description}

